    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. how does that presentation change when you're looking at patients with darker skin tones? like, are there different warning signs or different locations where it's more likely to show up compared to the standard case?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In patients with darker skin tones, melanoma is less likely to present as a new mole but often appears as a growing, changing spot on existing nevi, particularly on acral skin (palms, soles, and under nails) or the mucous membranes. While the ABCDE rule still applies, look for signs of acral melanoma such as black or dark blue discoloration on the nails or a non-healing blister-like lesion on the soles. Additionally, locally aggressive subtypes (like lentigo maligna) are more common on sun-exposed areas like the face, neck, and scalp in this demographic. Always remember that cryptic melanoma can be clustered under hair or appear on acral sites, requiring specific biopsies even if the lesion itself is subtle.",
